<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rajmahal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rajmahal is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> in the Sahibganj district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, at the gateway between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. In 1576 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> defeated the last Bengal sultan near it. Man Singh made it the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal-subah">Mughal Bengal</a> in 1595, under the name Akbarnagar, and it remained so, with interruptions, until the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a> in the early seventeenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rajmahal.jpg" alt="Rajmahal" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Prashant mandal · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hills_of_rajmahal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Rajmahal lies on the bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, with the Rajmahal hills on one side and the river on the other, in the Sahebganj district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a>. The area was earlier ruled by the Raja Mal sub-group of the Mal Paharia tribes, and the place was once known as Agmahal. During Mughal rule the region was also called Daman-i-Koh. Man Singh I chose the site for his capital in 1595–96 because it commanded the Teliagarhi Pass and the Ganges.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1576 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rajmahal">Battle of Rajmahal</a> was fought between the Mughal Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daud-khan-karrani">Daud Khan Karrani</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karrani-dynasty">Karrani dynasty</a>. Daud Khan was captured and later executed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>. After the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> in 1592, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/man-singh-i">Man Singh I</a>, Mughal governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal-subah">Bengal Subah</a>, came to Rajmahal, where he built a palace, a garden and a mosque and renamed the town Akbarnagar in 1595.</p><p>In 1610 Islam Khan moved the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> to Dacca, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a>. In 1639 Shah Shuja, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal-subah">Bengal Subah</a> and second son of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a>, made Rajmahal the capital again. It remained the seat of the Mughal viceroy until 1660 and a mint town until 1661. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plassey">Battle of Plassey</a> in 1757, Siraj-ud-Daulah halted at Rajmahal while fleeing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murshidabad">Murshidabad</a> towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a>. Mir Jafar's son Miran captured and murdered him there. In 1763 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-qasim">Mir Qasim</a> was defeated by the British at the Battle of Udhwa nala at Rajmahal.</p><p>Under British rule, Santals were increasingly used for land reclamation after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/permanent-settlement">Permanent Settlement</a> of 1793, and the British built the Neel Kothi to process and store <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a>. Daman-i-Koh became the origin of the Santhal Rebellion, in which leaders including Sidhu and Kanhu Murmu mobilised more than 10,000 Santhals against colonial authority and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindari</a> system.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Rajmahal has an area of 7.5 square kilometres. It had a population of 22,514 at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Its remains include the Teliagarhi Fort, called the ancient gateway of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, the Akbari Mosque, the palace of Shah Shuja with its marble pavilion Singhi Dalan facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, the tombs of Miran and of Maina-Bibi, and the Neel Kothi. The nearby plant-fossil beds of the Rajmahal Formation are a National Geological Monument.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rajmahal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
